"""A write has to name what the tree must say afterwards — whatever it reports (B5, B6). No Docker.

D16's re-read hung on the *report shape*: `person` and `bulk` had to name a person, and the five
tools that report nothing at all were held to nothing at all. `unguarded` returned from
`assert_self_report` before its first check, so removing the family PUT from
`add_child_to_family` left the whole suite green.

The mechanism was never missing — `expect_person` worked for any shape. The obligation was. And
the obligation could not simply be extended, because it only ever reached `people`: a family, a
note or a source has no `expect_person` to name. So the expectation is now
`{namespace: {gramps_id: {path: value}}}` over `by_gramps_id`, `expect_person` is the sugar for
the common case, and the one tool whose effect is genuinely not in the tree says so out loud.

The same re-read is what lets `assert_refusal` mean what its docstring says: counts and identity
cannot see a field written in place before the raise, and that is the worst refusal there is.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

